at's 12. So combining all digits:\n\nThe digits from right to left are 1 (ones), 0 (tens), 1 (hundreds), 7 (thousands), 2 (ten-thousands), and then 12 (hundred-thousands). Wait, but how does that form the number?\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me write it out step by step:\n\nLet me write each digit's place:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 1\n\nTens: 0 (with carryover 1)\n\nHundreds: 1 (with carryover 1)\n\nThousands: 7 (with carryover 1)\n\nTen-thousands: 2 (with carryover 1)\n\nHundred-thousands: 12 (so that's 2 in the hundred-thousands place and 1 in the millions place?)\n\nWait a minute, I think I made a mistake here. Because when adding the hundred-thousands place, which is the sixth digit from the right, the digits are 9 (from 997,871) and 2 (from 229,230). Adding those gives 9+2=11, plus the carryover 1 makes 12. So that's 2 in the hundred-thousands place and carryover 1 to the millions place. But since both original numbers are six-digit numbers, their sum could be a seven-digit number. So the total would be 1 (million) 2 (hundred thousand), then the rest.\n\nSo putting it all together:\n\nMillions place: 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 2\n\nTen-thousands: 2 (Wait, earlier I thought ten-thousands place was 2, but let me check again.\n\nWait, let's break down each digit's position properly.\n\nLet me write both numbers with their place values:\n\nFirst number: 997,871\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- Hundred thousands: 9\n\n- Ten thousands: 9\n\n- Thousands: 7\n\n- Hundreds: 8\n\n- Tens: 7\n\n- Ones: 1\n\nSecond number: 229,230\n\nBreaking down:\n\n- Hundred thousands: 2\n\n- Ten thousands: 2\n\n- Thousands: 9\n\n- Hundreds: 2\n\n- Tens: 3\n\n- Ones: 0\n\nWait, actually, maybe I misaligned the digits earlier. Let me confirm the place values properly.\n\nWait, 997,871 is:\n\nDigit positions (from right, starting at 0):\n\nPosition 0 (ones): 1\n\nPosition 1 (tens):7\n\nPosition 2 (hundreds):8\n\nPosition 3 (thousands):7\n\nPosition 4 (ten thousands):9\n\nPosition 5 (hundred thousands):9\n\nSimilarly, 229,230:\n\nPosition 0:0\n\nPosition 1:3\n\nPosition 2:2\n\nPosition 3:9\n\nPosition 4:2\n\nPosition 5:2\n\nSo when adding, let's go step by step:\n\nPosition 0 (ones): 1 + 0 =1\n\nPosition 1 (tens):7 +3=10 \u2192 0 carryover 1\n\nPosition 2 (hundreds):8 +2=10, plus carryover 1 \u219211 \u21921, carryover 1\n\nPosition 3 (thousands):7 +9=16, plus carryover 1 \u219217 \u21927, carryover1\n\nPosition4 (ten thousands):9 +2=11, plus carryover1 \u219212 \u21922, carryover1\n\nPosition5 (hundred thousands):9 +2=11, plus carryover1 \u219212 \u21922, carryover1\n\nNow, since there's a carryover of 1 after the hundred thousands place, we need to add a new digit at the millions place (position6): 1.\n\nSo the total sum is:\n\n1 (million) 2 (hundred thousand) 2 (ten thousand) 7 (thousand) 1 (hundred) 0 (ten) 1 (one). Wait, let me structure this correctly.\n\nWait, the digits from position0 to position6:\n\nPosition6:1\n\nPosition5:2\n\nPosition4:2\n\nPosition3:7\n\nPosition2:1\n\nPosition1:0\n\nPosition0:1\n\nSo writing the number from left to right (highest to lowest place):\n\n1 (million), 2 (hundred thousand), 2 (ten thousand), 7 (thousand), 1 (hundred), 0 (tens), 1 (ones). So that's 1,227,101.\n\nWait, but let me confirm with another method. Maybe adding the numbers directly:\n\n997,871\n\n+229,230\n\nLet me add them step by step:\n\nStart with 997,871 + 200,000 = 1,197,871\n\nThen add 29,230:\n\n1,197,871 + 29,230 = ?\n\n1,197,871 + 20,000 = 1,217,871\n\nThen +9,230: 1,217,871 +9,000 =1,226,871, then +230 =1,227,101. Yes, that matches.\n\nSo the final answer should be 1,227,101. Okay, so my initial calculation was correct. I just had to make sure I didn't mix up any digits during the place value breakdown. It's easy to miscount the positions, so going through each step carefully helps prevent errors.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the sum of 997871 and 229230?  \n# Reasoning:  \nLet's add the two numbers digit by digit.  \n