undred-thousands), and then the 1 in the millions place. So the total should be 1,087,034.\n\nWait a second, let me double-check that. Maybe I made a mistake in the carrying over. Let me go through each step again to confirm.\n\nStarting over:\n\nOnes: 3 + 1 = 4 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 8 + 5 = 13. Write 3, carry 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 3 + 6 = 9 + 1 (carry) = 10. Write 0, carry 1 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 4 + 2 = 6 + 1 (carry) = 7 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen-thousands: 5 + 3 = 8 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred-thousands: 6 + 4 = 10. So here, writing 0 and carrying over 1 to millions. So the final digits are 1 (millions), 0 (hundred-thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 7 (thousands), 0 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 4 (ones). Wait, that would make it 1,087,034. Hmm, that seems right. Let me add them another way to confirm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe breaking the numbers into parts. Let's see:\n\n654,383 + 432,651.\n\nBreaking down:\n\n600,000 + 50,000 + 4,000 + 300 + 80 + 3\n\nplus\n\n400,000 + 30,000 + 2,000 + 600 + 50 + 1\n\nAdding each part:\n\n600,000 + 400,000 = 1,000,000\n\n50,000 + 30,000 = 80,000\n\n4,000 + 2,000 = 6,000\n\n300 + 600 = 900\n\n80 + 50 = 130\n\n3 + 1 = 4\n\nNow add all those together:\n\n1,000,000 + 80,000 = 1,080,000\n\n1,080,000 + 6,000 = 1,086,000\n\n1,086,000 + 900 = 1,086,900\n\n1,086,900 + 130 = 1,087,030\n\n1,087,030 + 4 = 1,087,034\n\nOkay, that matches the previous result. So the sum is indeed 1,087,034. I think that's correct. Let me try using another method, like the standard column addition again to be thorough.\n\nStarting over:\n\n\u2003654383\n+432651\n---------\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 3 +1 =4\n\nTens:8+5=13, write 3, carry 1\n\nHundreds:3+6=9 +1=10, write 0, carry 1\n\nThousands:4+2=6 +1=7, no carry\n\nTen-thousands:5+3=8\n\nHundred-thousands:6+4=10, so write 0 and carry 1 to millions.\n\nSo the total is 1,087,034. Yep, same answer. I think that's solid. Maybe I can use a calculator to verify, but since I can't, the cross-checking methods both give