yover 1.\n\nThousands: 2 +0 =2, plus 1 carried over is 3. Write 3, no carryover.\n\nTen thousands: 9 +9 =18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\nHundred thousands: 4 +1 =5, plus the carried over 1 makes 6. So write 6.\n\nPutting all together: 6 (hundred thousands), 8 (ten thousands), 3 (thousands), 5 (hundreds), 7 (tens), 1 (ones). So the sum is 683,571?\n\nWait, let me check again. Wait, when adding the ten thousands place: 9 +9 is 18, so that's 8 in the ten thousands and carryover 1 to the hundred thousands. Then the hundred thousands digits are 4 +1 (from the original numbers) plus the carried over 1. So 4+1=5, plus 1 is 6. So yes, that's correct.\n\nWait, but let me verify with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\n492,904 + 190,667.\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n492,904 + 100,000 = 592,904\n\nThen add 90,000: 592,904 +90,000 = 682,904\n\nThen add 667: 682,904 +667 = 683,571. \n\nYes, that matches. So the answer should be 683,571. Let me confirm once more with standard addition:\n\n  492904\n+190667\n--------\nStarting from the right:\n\n4+7=11 \u2192 1, carry 1\n\n0+6=6 +1=7 \u2192 7, no carry\n\n9+6=15 \u21925, carry 1\n\n2+0=2 +1=3 \u21923, no carry\n\n9+9=18 \u21928, carry 1\n\n4+1=5 +1=6 \u21926\n\nSo writing all digits: 6 8 3 5 7 1 \u2192 683,571. Yep, that's correct.\n</think>\n\n",
