om the right:\n\nOnes: 1+1=2 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTens: 6+1=7 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundreds: 0+4=4 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nThousands: 4+9=13. Write 3, carryover 1. \u2714\ufe0f\n\nTen-thousands: 5+3 +1 (carry) =9 \u2714\ufe0f\n\nHundred-thousands: 6+4=10. Wait, but here, since there's no more digits beyond that, so 6 (from first number) plus 4 (from second) is 10. So that would be 0 in the hundred-thousands place and carryover 1 to the millions place. So the total becomes 1 (million) 0 (hundred thousand) 9 (ten thousand) 3 (thousand) 4 (hundred) 7 (ten) 2 (one). So 1,093,472. That seems right.\n\nWait, but let me do the addition another way to cross-verify. Maybe adding the numbers as whole numbers:\n\n654,061 + 439,411.\n\nLet me add 654,061 + 400,000 first, which is 1,054,061. Then add the remaining 39,411.\n\n1,054,061 + 39,411 = ?\n\nAdding 1,054,061 + 30,000 = 1,084,061\n\nThen +9,411: 1,084,061 + 9,000 = 1,093,061, then +411 gives 1,093,472. Yep, same result. Okay, that checks out. So the answer should be 1,093,472.\n</think>\n\n",
