arts. \n\nBreaking down 864,831 into 800,000 + 60,000 + 4,000 + 800 + 30 +1  \nAnd 105,986 into 100,000 + 5,000 + 900 + 80 +6. \n\nAdding the large parts first: 800,000 + 100,000 = 900,000  \nThen 60,000 + 5,000 = 65,000  \n4,000 + 900 = 4,900  \n800 + 80 = 880  \n30 +6 =36  \n1 remains. \n\nAdding all those together: 900,000 +65,000 = 965,000  \n965,000 +4,900 = 969,900  \n969,900 +880 = 970,780  \n970,780 +36 = 970,816  \nWait, that's 970,816? But earlier I had 970,817. Hmm, there's a discrepancy here. Which means I made a mistake somewhere. \n\nLet me check the breakdown again. \n\nOriginal numbers: 864,831 and 105,986. \n\nBreaking down 864,831:  \n800,000 + 60,000 + 4,000 + 800 + 30 +1. That's correct. \n\n105,986:  \n100,000 + 0 (since there's no digit in the ten-thousands place beyond the 1?) Wait, no. Wait, 105,986 is 1 hundred thousand, 0 ten thousands, 5 thousands, 9 hundreds, 8 tens, 6 ones. Wait, so the breakdown should be 100,000 + 5,000 + 900 + 80 +6. That's correct. \n\nAdding the parts again: \n\nHundred thousands: 800k +100k =900k.  \nTen thousands: 60k (from first number) + 0 (from second) =60k. Wait, hold on! Wait, in the breakdown, the first number's 60,000 is in the ten thousands place? Wait, no. Wait, 864,831 is 8 (hundred thousands), 6 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 1 (ones). So the 60,000 is indeed the ten thousands place. \n\nSo when adding the ten thousands place, the first number has 60,000 and the second has 0, so total 60,000. But in the breakdown method, when I added 800k +100k =900k, then 60k +5k (from the 5,000). Wait, no, that's where the mistake is. \n\nWait, the breakdown approach I did earlier was adding all the components separately, but perhaps I messed up the addition steps. Let me recalculate the breakdown addition properly:\n\nStarting with the largest parts:\n\nFirst number's components:  \n800,000  \n60,000  \n4,000  \n800  \n30  \n1  \n\nSecond number's components:  \n100,000  \n5,000  \n900  \n80 