 thousands, carry 1. \n\nHundred thousands: 2 +7 =9, plus the carried 1 makes 10. So 0 here and carry 1. Then the million's place is the carried 1. So the total is 1,064,866. Wait, but when I first thought, I had 10 in the hundred thousands place, so writing 0 and carrying over 1 to the millions. So the final number is 1,064,866. \n\nHmm, but let me verify using another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts. Let's break them down:\n\n276,312 can be 200,000 + 70,000 + 6,000 + 300 + 10 + 2\n\n788,554 is 700,000 + 80,000 + 8,000 + 500 + 50 +4\n\nAdding each part:\n\n200,000 + 700,000 = 900,000\n\n70,000 + 80,000 = 150,000\n\n6,000 +8,000 =14,000\n\n300 +500 =800\n\n10 +50 =60\n\n2 +4 =6\n\nNow add all those together:\n\n900,000 +150,000 =1,050,000\n\n1,050,000 +14,000 =1,064,000\n\n1,064,000 +800 =1,064,800\n\n1,064,800 +60 =1,064,860\n\n1,064,860 +6 =1,064,866\n\nOkay, that matches my previous result. So the sum is indeed 1,064,866. I think that's correct. Let me just do a quick check using estimation to see if it's in the ballpark. \n\n276,312 is approximately 276,000 and 788,554 is about 789,000. Adding those gives 276,000 +789,000 =1,065,000. The exact answer is 1,064,866, which is very close to the estimate, so that makes sense. The difference is because of rounding down the first number and up the second, but overall, it's in the right area. \n\nTherefore, I'm confident the answer is 1,064,866.\n</think>\n\n",
