 ensure accuracy.\n\nStarting over:\n\nNumbers:\n\n  926,399\n\n+254,485\n\nAligning:\n\n        9 2 6 3 9 9\n\n      + 2 5 4 4 8 5\n\nLet me write each digit's place:\n\nMillions: 0 (since both are six digits)\n\nHundred-thousands: 9 and 2\n\nTen-thousands: 2 and 5\n\nThousands: 6 and 4\n\nHundreds: 3 and 4\n\nTens: 9 and 8\n\nOnes: 9 and 5\n\nStarting from the right (ones):\n\n1. Ones: 9 +5 =14. Write 4, carryover 1.\n\n2. Tens: 9 +8 =17, plus 1 is 18. Write 8, carryover 1.\n\n3. Hundreds: 3 +4 =7, plus 1 is 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\n4. Thousands: 6 +4 =10. Write 0, carryover 1.\n\n5. Ten-thousands: 2 +5 =7, plus 1 is 8. Write 8, no carryover.\n\n6. Hundred-thousands: 9 +2 =11. Write 1, carryover 1.\n\n7. Millions place: The carried over 1. So that's 1.\n\nPutting all together:\n\nMillions: 1\n\nHundred-thousands: 1 (from 11, but actually, the 11 is in the hundred-thousands place, so the 1 is carried over to millions. Wait, let me see:\n\nWait, when adding the hundred-thousands digits (9 and 2), that's 11. So the sum here is 11, so we write down 1 in the hundred-thousands place and carry over 1 to the millions. Since there's no millions in the original numbers, the carried over 1 becomes the millions digit. So the total is 1,1 (million and hundred-thousand) followed by the other digits.\n\nSo the digits from left to right would be:\n\n1 (millions), 1 (hundred-thousands), 8 (ten-thousands), 0 (thousands), 8 (hundreds), 8 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSo the total is 1,180,884?\n\nWait, let me verify with another method. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nAlternatively, 926,399 + 254,485.\n\nLet me break it down:\n\n926,399 + 200,000 = 1,126,399\n\nThen add 54,485: 1,126,399 + 50,000 = 1,176,399\n\nThen +4,485: 1,176,399 +4,000 = 1,180,399\n\nThen +485: 1,180,399 +485 = 1,180,884. Okay, that matches.\n\nSo the final answer should be 1,180,884.\n\nWait, but in the initial step-by-step, when I added the hundred-thousands place (9+2=11), so that gives 11 in the hundred-thousands, which would be 1,100,000, but adding the carried over 1 from the ten-thousands place? Wait, no, let me retrace:\n\nWait, in step 5 (ten-thousands place):\n\nThe ten-thousands digits are 2 (from 926,399) and 5 (from 254,485). Adding those gives 2+5=7, plus the carryover from thousands place (which was 1 from the 10 in thousands place). So 7+1=8. So no carryover here.\n\nThen step 6 (hundred-thousands place): 9 +2 =11. So that's 11 in the hundred-thousands place. So we write 1 in the hundred-thousands and carry over 1 to millions. So the millions place is 1. So the total is 1,180,884. Yes, that's correct.\n\nSo the answer is 1,180,884. Let me check once more with another method, like column addition:\n\nLet me write it out vertically:\n\n      926399\n\n    +254485\n\n    ---------\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 9+5=14 \u2192 4, carry 1\n\nTens:9+8=17 +1=18 \u21928, carry1\n\nHundreds:3+4=7 +1=8 \u21928, no carry\n\nThousands:6+4=10 \u21920, carry1\n\nTen-thousands:2+5=7 +1=8 \u21928, no carry\n\nHundred-thousands:9+2=11 \u21921, carry1\n\nMillions: carry1 \u21921\n\nSo combining all digits: 1 1 8 0 8 8 4 \u2192 1,180,884. Yep, that's correct.\n\nI think that's solid. So the answer is 1,180,884.\n</think>\n\n",
