r has 9 (thousands place?), let me confirm the places:\n\nWait, maybe I messed up the place values. Let me write each digit's place clearly.\n\nLet me write both numbers digit by digit:\n\nFirst number: 4 (hundred thousands), 2 (ten thousands), 9 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 3 (tens), 4 (ones).\n\nSecond number: 2 (hundred thousands), 0 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 9 (hundreds), 9 (tens), 3 (ones).\n\nSo when adding:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\nOnes: 4 +3 =7.\n\nTens: 3 +9 =12 \u2192 2, carryover 1.\n\nHundreds:7 +9 =16 +1=17 \u21927, carryover1.\n\nThousands:9 (from first number) +4 (from second) =13 +1 (carryover) =14 \u21924, carryover1.\n\nTen thousands:2 (first) +0 (second) =2 +1 (carryover) =3.\n\nHundred thousands:4 +2 =6.\n\nSo putting all together: 6 (hundred thousands), 3 (ten thousands), 4 (thousands), 7 (hundreds), 2 (tens), 7 (ones). So 634,727. That seems right.\n\nWait, but let me add them another way to confirm. Maybe break them into parts.\n\n429,734 + 200,000 is 629,734. Then add 4,993. 629,734 +4,000 =633,734. Then +993: 633,734 +993 = 634,727. Yep, same result. Okay, so the answer should be 634,727.\n</think>\n\n",
