 write 1, carryover 1)\n6. Hundred-thousands:5 +1=6, plus the carryover 1 makes 7.\n\nSo the total should be 716,267?\n\nWait, let me add them numerically without breaking it down:\n\n543,125\n\n+173,142\n\nLet me add digit by digit:\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n5 +2 =7 (ones)\n\n2 +4 =6 (tens)\n\n1 +1 =2 (hundreds)\n\n3 +3 =6 (thousands)\n\n4 +7 =11 (ten thousands place: 1, carryover 1)\n\n5 +1 =6 plus the carryover 1 makes 7 (hundred thousands place)\n\nSo combining all the digits with the carryover, the result is 716,267.\n\nWait, but let me verify with another method to be sure. Maybe adding the numbers in parts.\n\nBreaking down 543,125 into 500,000 + 40,000 + 3,000 + 100 + 20 +5\n\nAnd 173,142 into 100,000 +70,000 +3,000 +100 +40 +2\n\nAdding each part:\n\n500,000 +100,000 =600,000\n\n40,000 +70,000 =110,000\n\n3,000 +3,000 =6,000\n\n100 +100 =200\n\n20 +40 =60\n\n5 +2 =7\n\nNow add all those together:\n\n600,000 +110,000 =710,000\n\n710,000 +6,000 =716,000\n\n716,000 +200 =716,200\n\n716,200 +60 =716,260\n\n716,260 +7 =716,267\n\nOkay, that matches the previous result. So the sum is indeed 716,267. I think that's correct. Let me just do a quick check using another method, maybe rounding.\n\nEstimate the numbers:\n\n543,125 is approximately 543,000\n\n173,142 is approximately 173,000\n\nAdding those gives 543,000 +173,000 =716,000. The actual sum is 716,267, which is close to the estimate, so that makes sense. The difference is 267, which comes from the rounded parts (125 +142 =267). So that checks out.\n\nTherefore, I'm confident the answer is 716,267.\n</think>\n\n",
